#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069943 :tutorial/content "The **product rule for radicals** states that the square root of a product equals the product of the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{ab} &=\\sqrt{a} \\cdot \\sqrt{b} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the product rule to rewrite the square roots of algebraic expressions.\n\nAs an example, let's suppose we want to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{32y}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite $32y$ as the product $16 \\cdot 2y$, which contains a perfect square $(16){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{32y} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{16 \\cdot 2y} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{4^2 \\cdot 2y}& \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we separate the product into two separate radicals using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{4^2\\cdot 2y} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{4^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}& \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{4^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\[3pt] &= \\mid 4 \\mid \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\[3pt] &= 4\\sqrt{2y}& \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "169256a7-1898-59b1-a7a9-f8cb8b713d51" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1606 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
